\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Set 4 - The Basic New Keynesian Model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Part*
Gali (2008), Chapter 3
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

There are several key elements of the baseline model that are a departure
 from the assumptions of the classical monetary economy.
 First, imperfect competition (i.e., monopolistic competition) in the goods
 market is introduced by assuming that each firm produces a differentiated
 good for which it sets the price (Dixit-Stiglite), instead of taking the
 price as given.
 Moreover, there are a continuum of goods.
 As a result, an individual firm can change its own price, but it has no
 affect on the aggregate price.
 Also, there is no strategic component between the firms.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Second, some constraints are imposed on the price adjustment mechanism by
 assuming that only a fraction of firms can reset their prices in any given
 period.
 In particular, and following much of the literature, a model of staggered
 price adjustment due to Calvo (1983) and characterized by random price
 durations is adopted.
 The resulting framework is referred to as the 
\emph on
basic New Keynesian model
\emph default
.
 In recent years, this model has become the workhorse for the analysis of
 monetary policy, fluctuations, and welfare.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The introduction of differentiated goods requires that the household problem
 be modified slightly relative to the one considered previously.
 That modification is discussed before turning to the firms' optimal price-setti
ng problem and the implied inflation dynamics.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Households
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Once again, assume a representative infinitely-lived household, seeking
 to maximize
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E_{0}\left\{ {\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\beta^{t}U\left(C_{t},N_{t}\right)\right\} 
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{t}$
\end_inset

 is now a consumption index given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
C_{t}\equiv\left[{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}C_{t}(i)^{1-\frac{1}{\epsilon}}di\right]^{\frac{\epsilon}{\epsilon-1}}
\]

\end_inset

with 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{t}(i)$
\end_inset

 representing the quantity of good 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 consumed by the household in period 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, while 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{t}$
\end_inset

 represents the total amount of goods consumed.
 As stated before, assume the existence of a continuum of goods represented
 by the interval 
\begin_inset Formula $[0,1]$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The period budget constraint now takes the form
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}P_{t}(i)C_{t}(i)di+Q_{t}B_{t}\leq B_{t-1}+W_{t}N_{t}+T_{t};\ \forall\ t=0,1,\ldots
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{t}(i)$
\end_inset

 is the price of good 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 in period 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{t}$
\end_inset

 denotes hours of work (or the measure of household members employed), and
 
\begin_inset Formula $W_{t}$
\end_inset

 is the nominal wage.
 In addition, 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{t}$
\end_inset

 represents purchases of one-period bonds (at a price 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{t}$
\end_inset

).
 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{t}>0$
\end_inset

 means that the household purchased bonds (i.e., it saved), and 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{t}<0$
\end_inset

 means that the household sold bonds (i.e., it borrowed).
 Finally, 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{t}$
\end_inset

 is a lump-sum component of income (which may include, among other items,
 dividends from ownership of firms).
 The above sequence of period budget constraints is supplemented with a
 non-Ponzi game condition of the form
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \lim_{T\rightarrow\infty}}E_{t}\left\{ B_{T}\right\} \geq0,\ \forall\ t.
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Notice that 
\begin_inset Formula $\{C_{t}(i)\}_{i\in[0,1]}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{t}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{t}$
\end_inset

 are the control variables of the model.
 We assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $U_{N,t}<0$
\end_inset

, so working causes disutility for the agent.
 For the consumer, 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon$
\end_inset

 can be thought of as the elasticity of substituion between the goods.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset

, then then consumer considers all the goods perfect substitutes.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon\rightarrow1$
\end_inset

, then the consumer considers all the goods perfect complements, i.e., he
 wants to consume each good in equal amounts.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We can 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

divide
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 the problem into two steps:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
{\displaystyle \max_{\{Z_{t},B_{t},N_{t}\}}} & E_{0}\left\{ {\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\beta^{t}U(C_{t}^{*},N_{t})\right\} \\
\mbox{s.t.} & Z_{t}+Q_{t}B_{t}=B_{t-1}+W_{t}N_{t}+T_{t}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{t}^{*}$
\end_inset

 is the solution of
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
{\displaystyle \max_{\{C_{t}(i)\}_{i\in[0,1]}}} & \left[{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}C_{t}(i)^{1-\frac{1}{\epsilon}}di\right]^{\frac{\epsilon}{\epsilon-1}}\\
\mbox{s.t.} & {\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}P_{t}(i)C_{t}(i)di=Z_{t}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Solving for 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{t}^{*}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

First, we wish to maximize the consumption index 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{t}$
\end_inset

 for any given level of expenditures 
\begin_inset Formula $\int_{0}^{1}P_{t}(i)C_{t}(i)$
\end_inset

.
 The objective funciton of this maximization problem is known as the constant
 elasticity of substitution (CES) aggregator function.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
{\displaystyle \max_{\{C_{t}(i)\}_{i\in[0,1]}}} & \left[{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}C_{t}(i)^{1-\frac{1}{\epsilon}}di\right]^{\frac{\epsilon}{\epsilon-1}}\\
\mbox{s.t.} & {\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}P_{t}(i)C_{t}(i)di=Z_{t}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Lets setup the Lagrangian
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
L=\left[{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}C_{t}(i)^{1-\frac{1}{\epsilon}}di\right]^{\frac{\epsilon}{\epsilon-1}}+\lambda\left[Z_{t}-{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}P_{t}(i)C_{t}(i)di\right]
\]

\end_inset

Important to note, in this construction, 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is just an index characterizing all the goods.
 Our goal is to optimize 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset

 with respect to a specific good (i.e., good 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

).
 Thus, the first-order condition of 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset

 with respect to good 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather}
\frac{\partial L}{\partial C_{t}(j)}=0\nonumber \\
\frac{\epsilon}{\epsilon-1}\left[{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}C_{t}(i)^{1-\frac{1}{\epsilon}}di\right]^{\frac{1}{\epsilon-1}}\frac{\epsilon-1}{\epsilon}C_{t}(j)^{-\frac{1}{\epsilon}}-\lambda P_{t}(j)=0\nonumber \\
\left[{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}C_{t}(i)^{1-\frac{1}{\epsilon}}di\right]^{\frac{1}{\epsilon-1}}\left[C_{t}(j)\right]^{-\frac{1}{\epsilon}}=\lambda P_{t}(j);\ \forall\ j\in[0,1]\nonumber \\
\left(\frac{C_{t}(j)}{C_{t}(k)}\right)^{-\frac{1}{\epsilon}}=\left(\frac{P_{t}(j)}{P_{t}(k)}\right);\ \forall\ k,j\in[0,1]\\
\log\left(\frac{C_{t}(j)}{C_{t}(k)}\right)=-\epsilon\log\left(\frac{P_{t}(j)}{P_{t}(k)}\right);\ \forall\ k,j\in[0,1]\nonumber \\
-\frac{d\log\left(\frac{C_{t}(j)}{C_{t}(k)}\right)}{d\log\left(\frac{P_{t}(j)}{P_{t}(k)}\right)}=\epsilon
\end{gather}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The left hand side of equation (2) is known as the elasticity of substitution
 between every pair of products.
 As one can see, the elasticity of substitution in the Dixit-Stiglitz economy
 is constant, which is not very realistic, but it makes the derivations
 much simpler.
 With a large 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon$
\end_inset

, the agent does not care about mixing because he only wishes to consume
 the goods that are relatively cheaper.
 As a result, demand is very responsive to a price change.
 With a small 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon$
\end_inset

, the agent wishes to diversify his consumption independent of the price.
 Hence, demand is not very responsive to a price change.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

From equation (1), we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
C_{t}(j)=C_{t}(k)\left(\frac{P_{t}(j)}{P_{t}(k)}\right)^{-\epsilon};\ \forall\ j,k\in[0,1]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Let us substitute equation (3) into the constraint.
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}P_{t}(j)C_{t}(j)dj & = & Z_{t}\nonumber \\
{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}P_{t}(j)\left(\frac{P_{t}(j)}{P_{t}(k)}\right)^{-\epsilon}C_{t}(k)dj & = & Z_{t}\nonumber \\
C_{t}(k)\left[P_{t}(k)\right]^{\epsilon}{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}\left[P_{t}(j)\right]^{1-\epsilon}d_{j} & = & Z_{t}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

Now, we define the Dixit-Stiglitx aggregate price index 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{t}$
\end_inset

 as 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P_{t}\equiv\left[{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}P_{t}(j)^{1-\epsilon}d_{j}\right]^{\frac{1}{1-\epsilon}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{t}^{1-\epsilon}\equiv{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}P_{t}(j)^{1-\epsilon}d_{j}$
\end_inset

, then equation (4) becomes
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
C_{t}(k)\left[P_{t}(k)\right]^{\epsilon}P_{t}^{1-\epsilon} & = & Z_{t}\nonumber \\
C_{t}(k) & = & \left(\frac{P_{t}(k)}{P_{t}}\right)^{-\epsilon}\frac{Z_{t}}{P_{t}}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

which is a necessary condition for maximizing the consumption index.
 Next, we substitute equation (6) into the consumption idex.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\left[{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}C_{t}(k)^{1-\frac{1}{\epsilon}}dk\right]^{\frac{\epsilon}{\epsilon-1}} & = & \left\{ {\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}\left[\left(\frac{P_{t}(k)}{P_{t}}\right)^{-\epsilon}\left(\frac{Z_{t}}{P_{t}}\right)\right]^{1-\frac{1}{\epsilon}}dk\right\} ^{\frac{\epsilon}{\epsilon-1}}\nonumber \\
 & = & \left\{ \left[\left(\frac{1}{P_{t}}\right)^{-\epsilon}\left(\frac{Z_{t}}{P_{t}}\right)\right]^{\frac{\epsilon-1}{\epsilon}}{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}\left(P_{t}(k)^{-\epsilon}\right)^{\frac{\epsilon-1}{\epsilon}}dk\right\} ^{\frac{\epsilon}{\epsilon-1}}\nonumber \\
 & = & \left(\frac{1}{P_{t}}\right)^{-\epsilon}\left(\frac{Z_{t}}{P_{t}}\right)\left[{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}P_{t}(k)^{1-\epsilon}dk\right]^{\frac{\epsilon}{\epsilon-1}}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

Finally, from the aggregate price index, equation (7) yields
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather}
\left(\frac{1}{P_{t}}\right)^{-\epsilon}\left(\frac{Z_{t}}{P_{t}}\right)P_{t}^{-\epsilon}=\frac{Z_{t}}{P_{t}}
\end{gather}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To summarize our derivations, the solution to the problem
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
{\displaystyle \max_{\{C_{t}(i)\}_{i\in[0,1]}}} & \left[{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}C_{t}(i)^{1-\frac{1}{\epsilon}}di\right]^{\frac{\epsilon}{\epsilon-1}}\\
\mbox{s.t.} & {\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}P_{t}(i)C_{t}(i)di=Z_{t}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

yields the set of demand equations
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
C_{t}(i)=\left(\frac{P_{t}(i)}{P_{t}}\right)^{-\epsilon}\frac{Z_{t}}{P_{t}};\ \forall\ i\in[0,1]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and the maximized level of the consumption basket
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
C_{t}^{*}=\frac{Z_{t}}{P_{t}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\strikeout off
\uuline off
\uwave off
\noun off
\color none

\begin_inset Formula $P_{t}\equiv\left[\int_{0}^{1}P_{t}(i)^{1-\epsilon}d_{j}\right]^{\frac{1}{1-\epsilon}}$
\end_inset

 is the aggregate price index
\family default
\series default
\shape default
\size default
\emph default
\bar default
\strikeout default
\uuline default
\uwave default
\noun default
\color inherit
.
 Furthermore, and conditional on such optimal behavior,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
{\displaystyle \int}_{0}^{1}P_{t}(i)C_{t}(i)di=P_{t}C_{t}^{*}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

i.e., total consumption expenditures can be written as the product of the
 price index times the quantity index.
 Now, we can plug equations (9) and (10) it into the household's constrained
 utility maximization problem.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
The Households' Problem Revisited
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In the previous subsection, we found the set of demand equations for each
 individual good, the maximized consumption index, and total consumption
 expenditure.
 With this information, the household no longer has to worry about choosing
 its consumption for each individual good.
 Instead, it chooses its consumption basket as a whole.
 Thus, its constrained maximization problem becomes
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
{\displaystyle \max_{\{C_{t},B_{t},N_{t}\}}\ } & E_{0}\left\{ \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}\beta^{t}U(C_{t},N_{t})\right\} \\
\mbox{s.t.} & P_{t}C_{t}+Q_{t}B_{t}=B_{t-1}+W_{t}N_{t}+T_{t}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

which is formally identical to the problem faced by the households in the
 single good economy analyzed in Set 3 of the lecture notes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

After setting up the Lagrangian
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
L=E_{0}\left\{ {\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\beta^{t}\left[U(C_{t},N_{t})+\lambda_{t}\left(-P_{t}C_{t}-Q_{t}B_{t}+B_{t-1}+W_{t}N_{t}+T_{t}\right)\right]\right\} 
\]

\end_inset

we obtain the following first-order conditions
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
U_{c,t}-\lambda_{t}P_{t} & = & 0\\
U_{n,t}+\lambda_{t}W_{t} & = & 0\\
\lambda_{t}(-Q_{t})+E_{t}\left\{ \beta\lambda_{t+1}\right\}  & = & 0
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Equations (12) and (13) imply
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
-U_{n,t}=\left(\frac{W_{t}}{P_{t}}\right)U_{c,t}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The LHS is the marginal disutility of labor.
 The RHS is the real wage times the marginal utility of consumption.
 So, the marignal cost of working equals the marginal benefit of working.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Equations (12) and (14) imply
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
Q_{t} & = & E_{t}\left\{ \beta\frac{U_{c,t+1}}{U_{c,t}}\frac{P_{t}}{P_{t+1}}\right\} \nonumber \\
\frac{Q_{t}}{P_{t}}U_{c,t} & = & E_{t}\left\{ \beta\frac{U_{c,t+1}}{P_{t+1}}\right\} 
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

Note that 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{t}$
\end_inset

 is the nominal price of a bond.
 So, the LHS is the real price of the bond times the marginal utility of
 consumption; it is what the household would receive by selling a bond.
 The RHS is the expected discounted marginal benefit of consumption the
 household gives up in 
\begin_inset Formula $t+1$
\end_inset

 by selling the bond.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Under the assumption of a period utility given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
U(C_{t},N_{t})=\frac{C_{t}^{1-\sigma}}{1-\sigma}-\frac{N_{t}^{1+\varphi}}{1+\varphi}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

the first-order conditions become
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
N_{t}^{\varphi} & = & \left(\frac{W_{t}}{P_{t}}\right)C_{t}^{-\sigma}\\
Q_{t} & = & E_{t}\left\{ \beta\left(\frac{C_{t+1}}{C_{t}}\right)^{-\sigma}\frac{P_{t}}{P_{t+1}}\right\} 
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

Condition (18) can be interpreted as a competitive labor supply schedule,
 determining the quantity of labor supplied as a function of the real wage,
 given the marginal utility of consumption (which under the assumptions
 is a function of consumption only).
 We will return to the household's problem when we study general equilibrium.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Firms
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

A continuum of firms indexed by 
\begin_inset Formula $i\in[0,1]$
\end_inset

.
 Each firm produces a differentiated good, but they all use an identical
 technology, represented by the production function
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
Y_{t}(i)=A_{t}N_{t}(i)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{t}$
\end_inset

 represents the level of technology assumed to be common to all firms and
 to evolve exogenously over time.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

All firms face an identical isoelastic demand schedule given by (9), and
 take the aggregate price level 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{t}$
\end_inset

 and aggregate consumption index 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{t}$
\end_inset

 as given.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Following the formalism proposed in Calvo (1983), each firm may reset its
 price only with probability 
\begin_inset Formula $1-\theta$
\end_inset

 in any given period, independent of the time elapsed since the last adjustment.
 So, 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 is the probability that a firm's price will remain the same in any given
 period.
 Thus, each period a measure of 
\begin_inset Formula $1-\theta$
\end_inset

 producers reset their prices, while a fraction 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 keep their prices unchanged.
 As a result, the average duration of a price is given by 
\begin_inset Formula $(1-\theta)^{-1}$
\end_inset

.
 In this context, 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 becomes a natural index of price stickiness.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Optimal Price Setting
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Firm 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 reoptimizing in period 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 will choose its price 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{t}^{*}$
\end_inset

 that maximizes the current market value of its profits generated while
 that price remains effective.
 Formally, it solves the problem
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \max_{P_{t}^{*}}{\displaystyle \sum_{k=0}^{\infty}}}\theta^{k}E_{t}\left\{ Q_{t,t+k}\left(P_{t}^{*}Y_{t+k|t}-\frac{W_{t+k}}{A_{t+k}}Y_{t+k|t}\right)\right\} 
\]

\end_inset

subject to the sequence of demand constraints
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
Y_{t+k|t}=\left(\frac{P_{t}^{*}}{P_{t+k}}\right)^{-\epsilon}C_{t+k};\ \forall\ k=0,1,\ldots
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
Q_{t,t+k}\equiv\beta^{k}\left(\frac{C_{t+k}}{C_{t}}\right)^{-\sigma}\frac{P_{t}}{P_{t+k}}
\]

\end_inset

is the stochastic discount factor for nominal payoffs, 
\begin_inset Formula $Y_{t+k|t}$
\end_inset

 denotes output in period 
\begin_inset Formula $t+k$
\end_inset

 for a firm that last reset its price in period 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{t}$
\end_inset

 is the Dixit-Stiglitx aggregate price index (it is the general price level
 faced by all the firms in period 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

), and 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{t}^{*}$
\end_inset

 is the optimal price set in period 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 by firms reoptimizing their price in that period.
 Notice that, as shown above, all firms will choose the same price because
 they face an identical problem.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The first-order condition associated with the problem above takes the form
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
{\displaystyle \sum_{k=0}^{\infty}}\theta^{k}E_{t}\left\{ Q_{t,t+k}\left(Y_{t+k|t}+P_{t}^{*}\frac{\partial Y_{t+k|t}}{\partial P_{t}^{*}}-\frac{W_{t+k}}{A_{t+k}}\frac{\partial Y_{t+k|t}}{\partial P_{t}^{*}}\right)\right\}  & = & 0\\
{\displaystyle \sum_{k=0}^{\infty}}\theta^{k}E_{t}\left\{ Q_{t,t+k}\left[Y_{t+k|t}+\left(P_{t}^{*}-\frac{W_{t+k}}{A_{t+k}}\right)\frac{\partial Y_{t+k|t}}{\partial P_{t}^{*}}\right]\right\}  & = & 0\\
{\displaystyle \sum_{k=0}^{\infty}}\theta^{k}E_{t}\left\{ Q_{t,t+k}\left[Y_{t+k|t}+\left(P_{t}^{*}-\frac{W_{t+k}}{A_{t+k}}\right)\left(-\epsilon\right)\frac{Y_{t+k|t}}{P_{t}^{*}}\right]\right\}  & = & 0\\
{\displaystyle \sum_{k=0}^{\infty}}\theta^{k}E_{t}\left\{ Q_{t,t+k}Y_{t+k|t}\left(1-\epsilon+\epsilon\frac{1}{P_{t}^{*}}\frac{W_{t+k}}{A_{t+k}}\right)\right\}  & = & 0
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Multiplying both sides by 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{t}^{*}\frac{1}{1-\epsilon}$
\end_inset

 yields 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
{\displaystyle \sum_{k=0}^{\infty}}\theta^{k}E_{t}\left\{ Q_{t,t+k}Y_{t+k|t}\left(P_{t}^{*}-M\frac{W_{t+k}}{A_{t+k}}\right)\right\} =0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{W_{t+k}}{A_{t+k}}$
\end_inset

 is the nominal marginal cost in period 
\begin_inset Formula $t+k$
\end_inset

 for a firm which last reset its price in period 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $M\equiv\frac{\epsilon}{\epsilon-1}>1$
\end_inset

.
 This is known as the optimal price setting condition.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Note that in the limiting case of no price rigidities 
\begin_inset Formula $(\theta=0)$
\end_inset

, each firm reoptimizes every period.
 Then, the previous condition collapses to the familiar optimal price-setting
 condition under flexible prices
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P_{t}^{*}-M\frac{W_{t}}{A_{t}}=0\iff P_{t}^{*}=M\frac{W_{t}}{A_{t}}
\]

\end_inset

which allows us to interpret 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

 as the desired markup in the absense of constraints on the frequency of
 price adjustment.
 Henceforth, 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

 is referred to as the desired, or frictionless, gross markup.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Aggregate Price Dynamics
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

From the above environment, we can derive an equation that describes the
 aggregate price dynamics.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
P_{t} & = & \left[{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}P_{t}(i)^{1-\epsilon}di\right]^{\frac{1}{1-\epsilon}}\\
 & = & \left[(1-\theta)\left(P_{t}^{*}\right)^{1-\epsilon}+{\displaystyle \int_{i\in S(t)}}P_{t-1}(i)^{1-\epsilon}di\right]^{\frac{1}{1-\epsilon}}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $S(t)\subset[0,1]$
\end_inset

 is the set of firms that are not reoptimizing their price in period 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{t}^{*}$
\end_inset

 is the same for all the firms reoptimizing their price in period 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
P_{t} & = & \left[(1-\theta)\left(P_{t}^{*}\right)^{1-\epsilon}+\theta\left(P_{t-1}\right)^{1-\epsilon}\right]^{\frac{1}{1-\epsilon}}\nonumber \\
P_{t} & = & \left[\theta\left(P_{t-1}\right)^{1-\epsilon}+(1-\theta)\left(P_{t}^{*}\right)^{1-\epsilon}\right]^{\frac{1}{1-\epsilon}}\nonumber \\
P_{t}^{1-\epsilon} & = & \theta\left(P_{t-1}\right)^{1-\epsilon}+(1-\theta)\left(P_{t}^{*}\right)^{1-\epsilon}\nonumber \\
\Pi_{t}^{1-\epsilon} & = & \theta+(1-\theta)\left(\frac{P_{t}^{*}}{P_{t-1}}\right)^{1-\epsilon}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi_{t}\equiv\frac{P_{t}}{P_{t-1}}$
\end_inset

 is gross inflation rate between 
\begin_inset Formula $t-1$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 Note that aggregate price dynamics are described by equation (23).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Equilibrium
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

There are two market clearing conditions:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Market clearing in the goods market requires
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
Y_{t}(i)=C_{t}(i)
\]

\end_inset

for all 
\begin_inset Formula $i\in[0,1]$
\end_inset

 and all 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 Letting aggregate output be defined as 
\begin_inset Formula $Y_{t}\equiv\left[\int_{0}^{1}Y_{t}(i)^{1-\frac{1}{\epsilon}}di\right]^{\frac{\epsilon}{\epsilon-1}}$
\end_inset

 it follows that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
Y_{t}=C_{t}
\]

\end_inset

must hold for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 Aggregate supply equals aggregate consumption.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Market clearing in the labor market requires
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
N_{t}={\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}N_{t}(i)di
\]

\end_inset

for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 The hours of work chosen by the households equals the hours of work demanded
 by firms.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Using the optimality conditions of the household and firms, along with the
 market clearing conditions, the economy can be summarized by the following
 seven equations.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
N_{t}^{\varphi}=\frac{W_{t}}{P_{t}}C_{t}^{-\sigma}\\
Q_{t}=E_{t}\left\{ \beta\left(\frac{C_{t+1}}{C_{t}}\right)^{-\sigma}\frac{P_{t}}{P_{t+1}}\right\} \\
{\displaystyle \sum_{k=0}^{\infty}}\theta^{k}E_{t}\left\{ Q_{t,t+k}Y_{t+k|t}\left(P_{t}^{*}-M\frac{W_{t+k}}{A_{t+k}}\right)\right\} =0\\
\Pi_{t}^{1-\epsilon}=\theta+(1-\theta)\left(\frac{P_{t}^{*}}{P_{t-1}}\right)^{1-\epsilon}\\
Y_{t}=C_{t}\\
N_{t}={\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}N_{t}(i)di\\
Q_{t}\ \mbox{is set be the central bank}
\end{gather*}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Now, we will study the dynamics of this economy by simplifying the above
 system of equations, performing log-linearization on the equations that
 remain, and then analyzing the log-linearized equations in Dynare.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Simplification
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Using the competitive labor supply schedule (equation 18)
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
N_{t}^{\varphi} & = & \left(\frac{W_{t}}{P_{t}}\right)C_{t}^{-\sigma}\\
W_{t} & = & N_{t}^{\varphi}C_{t}^{\sigma}P_{t}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

and the market clearing condition for the goods market, the above system
 of seven equations can be simplified.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather}
Q_{t}=E_{t}\left\{ \beta\left(\frac{C_{t+1}}{C_{t}}\right)^{-\sigma}\frac{1}{\Pi_{t+1}}\right\} \\
{\displaystyle \sum_{k=0}^{\infty}}\theta^{k}E_{t}\left\{ Q_{t,t+k}Y_{t+k|t}\left[\frac{P_{t}^{*}}{P_{t-1}}-M\left(\frac{N_{t+k}^{\varphi}Y_{t+k}^{\sigma}}{A_{t+k}}\right)\Pi_{t-1,t+k}\right]\right\} =0\\
\Pi_{t}^{1-\epsilon}=\theta+(1-\theta)\left(\frac{P_{t}^{*}}{P_{t-1}}\right)^{1-\epsilon}\\
N_{t}={\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}N_{t}(i)di\\
Q_{t}\ \mbox{is set by the central bank}
\end{gather}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi_{t-1,t+k}\equiv\nicefrac{P_{t+k}}{P_{t-1}}$
\end_inset

.
 We now have a system of 5 equations and 5 endogenous variables 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(Q_{t},Y_{t},\Pi_{t},\frac{P_{t}^{*}}{P_{t-1}},N_{t}\right)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Benchmark Case
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

As stated previously, a useful benchmark is the case of flexible prices
 
\begin_inset Formula $(\theta=0)$
\end_inset

.
 Our focus is on a zero inflation, steady state equilibrium.
 It follows from (26) that in a steady state equilibrium with zero inflation
 (i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi=1$
\end_inset

),
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Pi_{t}=1\iff\frac{P_{t}^{*}}{P_{t-1}}=1
\]

\end_inset

so 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{t}^{*}=P_{t-1}=P_{t}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 Then, at 
\begin_inset Formula $k=0$
\end_inset

, equation (25) implies
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
Y_{t|t}\left(1-M\frac{N_{t}^{\varphi}Y_{t}^{\sigma}}{A_{t}}\right)=0\iff MN_{t}^{\varphi}Y_{t}^{\sigma}=A_{t}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Also, since all firms are symmetric, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
Y_{t} & = & \left[{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}Y_{t}(i)^{1-\frac{1}{\epsilon}}di\right]^{\frac{\epsilon}{\epsilon-1}}\ \mbox{(definition)}\nonumber \\
 & = & \left[{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}\left(A_{t}N_{t}(i)\right)^{1-\frac{1}{\epsilon}}di\right]^{\frac{\epsilon}{\epsilon-1}}\ \mbox{(definition)}\nonumber \\
 & = & \left[{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}\left(A_{t}N_{t}\right)^{1-\frac{1}{\epsilon}}di\right]^{\frac{\epsilon}{\epsilon-1}}\ \mbox{(symmetry)}\nonumber \\
 & = & A_{t}N_{t}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Thus, from equations (29) and (30), we have 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
M\left(\frac{Y_{t}}{A_{t}}\right)^{\varphi}Y_{t}^{\sigma} & = & A_{t}\nonumber \\
Y_{t}^{\sigma+\varphi} & = & \frac{1}{M}A_{t}^{1+\varphi}\nonumber \\
Y_{t} & = & \left[\frac{1}{M}A_{t}^{1+\varphi}\right]^{\frac{1}{\varphi+\sigma}}\equiv Y_{t}^{n}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $Y_{t}^{n}$
\end_inset

 is known as the natural level of output.
 It is the equilibrium level of output under flexible prices, and it is
 determined independently of monetary policy (again).
 This is why we will introduce 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

price stickiness.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The level of output that solves the social planner's problem
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
{\displaystyle \max_{\{C_{t}(i),N_{t}(i)\}_{i\in[0,1],t=0,1,\ldots}}} & E_{0}\left\{ {\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\beta^{t}\left(\frac{C_{t}^{1-\sigma}}{1-\sigma}-\frac{N_{t}^{1+\varphi}}{1+\varphi}\right)\right\} \\
\mbox{s.t.} & C_{t}=\left[{\displaystyle \intop_{0}^{1}}C_{t}(i)^{1-\frac{1}{\epsilon}}di\right]^{\frac{\epsilon}{\epsilon-1}}\\
 & N_{t}={\displaystyle \intop_{0}^{1}}N_{t}(i)di\\
 & C_{t}(i)=A_{t}N_{t}(i)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

is the socially efficient level of output.
 In Homework 6, it was shown that this optimal level of output is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
C_{t}^{*}=A_{t}^{\frac{1+\varphi}{\varphi+\sigma}}\equiv Y_{t}^{e}
\]

\end_inset

Notice that when 
\begin_inset Formula $M=1$
\end_inset

 (perfect competition), the natural level of output corresponds to the equilibri
um level of ouput in the classial economy, as derived in the Set 3 notes.
 The presence of market power by firms has the effect of lowering that output
 level uniformly over time, without affecting its sensitivity to changes
 in technology.
 Thus, because of markup power, 
\begin_inset Formula $Y_{t}^{n}$
\end_inset

 is less than the socially efficient level of output.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
The 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Sticky Price
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 Model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

As stated previously, we have a simplified the sticky price model into a
 system of 5 equations (equations 24 through 28) and 5 endogenous variables
 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(Q_{t},Y_{t},\Pi_{t},\frac{P_{t}^{*}}{P_{t-1}},N_{t}\right)$
\end_inset

.
 After some tedious calculation, we can reduce our current system into a
 brand new one with only 3 equations and 3 endogenous variables 
\begin_inset Formula $(\pi_{t},\tilde{y}_{t},i_{t})$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{y}_{t}\equiv y_{t}-y_{t}^{n}$
\end_inset

 is the log deviation of output from its flexible price counterpart, and
 following convention, it is referred to as the 
\series bold
output gap
\series default
.
 Important to note, the lower case variables represent the 
\begin_inset Formula $\log$
\end_inset

 values of their upper case counterparts (i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{t}\equiv\log X_{t}$
\end_inset

).
 Using equation (24), we will derive the 
\series bold
Dynamic IS Equation
\series default
, and using equations 24 through 27, we will derive the 
\series bold
New Keynesian Phillips Curve
\series default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Log-Linearization
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Dynamic IS Equation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To begin, we rewrite equation (24) as follows
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
Q_{t} & = & E_{t}\left\{ \beta\left(\frac{Y_{t+1}}{Y_{t}}\right)^{-\sigma}\frac{1}{\Pi_{t+1}}\right\} \\
\exp(-i_{t}) & = & E_{t}\left\{ \exp\left[-\rho-\sigma(y_{t+1}-y_{t})-\pi_{t+1}\right]\right\} 
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $i_{t}\equiv-\log Q_{t}$
\end_inset

 is the is nominal interest rate, 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi_{t}\equiv\log\Pi_{t}$
\end_inset

 is the inflation rate, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho\equiv-\log\beta$
\end_inset

 is the real interest rate in the steady state.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*
Here is a numerical example concerning the nature of the different variables.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{t}=0.95$
\end_inset

; 
\begin_inset Formula $\log Q_{t}\approx-0.0513$
\end_inset

; 
\begin_inset Formula $i_{t}\equiv-\log Q_{t}\approx0.0513$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*
\begin_inset Formula $P_{t}=1.5$
\end_inset

; 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{t-1}=1$
\end_inset

; 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi_{t}=\nicefrac{P_{t}}{P_{t-1}}=1.5$
\end_inset

; 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi_{t}\equiv\log\Pi_{t}=0.4054651081$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Now, we log-linearize the new version of equation (24) around the steady
 state.
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
-\hat{i}_{t}=E_{t}\left\{ \left[-\sigma(\hat{y}_{t+1}-\hat{y}_{t})-\pi_{t+1}\right]\right\} 
\]

\end_inset

Important to note, the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

hat
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 over the lower-case variables represents the deviation of the variable
 from its steady state (i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}_{t}\equiv x_{t}-x_{ss}$
\end_inset

).
 So, we have 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{i}_{t}=i_{t}-i_{ss}$
\end_inset

.
 Since there is no inflation in the steady state (i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi_{t}=0$
\end_inset

), then 
\begin_inset Formula $i_{ss}=\rho$
\end_inset

.
 Moreover, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{y}_{t+1}-\hat{y}=y_{t+1}-y-y_{t}+y=y_{t+1}-y_{t}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, we can rewrite the equation above as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
-(i_{t}-\rho) & = & E_{t}\left\{ -\sigma(y_{t+1}-y_{t})-\pi_{t+1}\right\} \nonumber \\
y_{t} & = & E_{t}\left\{ y_{t+1}\right\} -\frac{1}{\sigma}\left(i_{t}-E_{t}\{\pi_{t+1}\}-\rho\right)\nonumber \\
y_{t}-y_{t}^{n} & = & E_{t}\left\{ y_{t+1}-y_{t+1}^{n}\right\} -\frac{1}{\sigma}\left(i_{t}-E_{t}\left\{ \pi_{t+1}\right\} -\rho\right)-y_{t}^{n}+E_{t}\left\{ y_{t+1}^{n}\right\} \nonumber \\
y_{t}-y_{t}^{n} & = & E_{t}\left\{ y_{t+1}-y_{t+1}^{n}\right\} -\frac{1}{\sigma}\left[i_{t}-E_{t}\left\{ \pi_{t+1}\right\} -\rho+\sigma\left(y_{t}^{n}-E_{t}\left\{ y_{t+1}^{n}\right\} \right)\right]\nonumber \\
\tilde{y}_{t} & = & E_{t}\left\{ \tilde{y}_{t+1}\right\} -\frac{1}{\sigma}\left[i_{t}-E_{t}\left\{ \pi_{t+1}\right\} -r_{t}^{n}\right]
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{y}_{t}\equiv y_{t}-y_{t}^{n}$
\end_inset

 is the output gap and 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{t}^{n}$
\end_inset

 is the 
\series bold
natural rate of interest
\series default
 given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
r_{t}^{n} & = & \rho-\sigma\left(y_{t}^{n}-E_{t}\left\{ y_{t+1}^{n}\right\} \right)\nonumber \\
 & = & \rho+\sigma\left(E_{t}\left\{ y_{t+1}^{n}\right\} -y_{t}^{n}\right)\nonumber \\
r_{t}^{n} & = & \rho+\sigma E_{t}\left\{ \Delta y_{t+1}^{n}\right\} 
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Henceforth, equation (32) is known as the 
\series bold
Dynamic IS Equation 
\series default
(or DIS, for short).
 It was shown that the natural level of output, 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}^{n}$
\end_inset

, is a linear function of technology shock, so it is an exogenous variable.
 Hence, the natural rate of interest, 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{t}^{n}$
\end_inset

, is also exogenous.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Remember, in a steady state with zero inflation, 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}^{n}$
\end_inset

 is the equilibrium level of output under flexible prices.
 Because of monopoly power, the natural rate of output is strictly less
 than the efficient rate of output, 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}^{e}$
\end_inset

, while the actual rate of output, 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}$
\end_inset

, fluctuates around 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}^{n}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Under the assumption that the effects of nominal rigidities vanish asymptoticall
y, 
\begin_inset Formula $\lim_{T\rightarrow\infty}E_{t}\left\{ \tilde{y}_{t+T}\right\} =0$
\end_inset

.
 In that case one can solve equation (32) forward to yield the expression
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\tilde{y}_{t}=-\frac{1}{\sigma}{\displaystyle \sum_{k=0}^{\infty}}(r_{t+k}-r_{t+k}^{n})
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{t}\equiv i_{t}-E_{t}\left\{ \pi_{t+1}\right\} $
\end_inset

 is the expected real return on a one period bond (i.e., the real interest
 rate).
 The previous expression emphasizes the fact that the output gap is proportional
 to the sum of current and anticipated deviations between the real interest
 rate and its natural counterpart.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
New Keynesian Phillips Curve
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Continuing with the derivations, we log-linearize equation (25), i.e., the
 optimal price-setting condition,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \sum_{k=0}^{\infty}}\theta^{k}E_{t}\left\{ Q_{t,t+k}Y_{t+k|t}\left[\frac{P_{t}^{*}}{P_{t-1}}-M\left(\frac{N_{t+k}^{\varphi}Y_{t+k}^{\sigma}}{A_{t+k}}\right)\Pi_{t-1,t+k}\right]\right\} =0
\]

\end_inset

around the zero inflation steady state.
 In the zero inflation steady state, 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{P_{t}^{*}}{P_{t-1}}=1$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi_{t-1,t+k}=1$
\end_inset

.
 Furthermore, constancy of the price level implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{t}^{*}=P_{t+k}$
\end_inset

 in that steady state, from which it follows that 
\begin_inset Formula $Y_{t+k|t}=Y$
\end_inset

 and
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{N_{t+k}^{\varphi}Y_{t+k}^{\sigma}}{A_{t+k}}=\frac{N^{\varphi}Y^{\sigma}}{A}
\]

\end_inset

because all firms will be producing the same quantity of output.
 In addition, 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{t,t+k}=\beta^{k}$
\end_inset

 must hold in that steady state.
 Let us focus on the expression within the expectation.
 We apply a first-order Taylor approximation of equation (25) around the
 zero inflation steady state.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
Q_{t,t+k}Y_{t+k|t}\left[\frac{P_{t}^{*}}{P_{t-1}}-M\left(\frac{N_{t+k}^{\varphi}Y_{t+k}^{\sigma}}{A_{t+k}}\right)\Pi_{t-1,t+k}\right]\approx\beta^{k}Y\left[1-M\left(\frac{N^{\varphi}Y^{\sigma}}{A}\right)\right]\\
+(\mbox{first order terms of \ensuremath{Q_{t+k}Y_{t+k|t}})}\left[1-M\left(\frac{N^{\varphi}Y^{\sigma}}{A}\right)\right]\\
+\beta^{k}Y\left[\log\frac{P_{t}^{*}}{P_{t-1}}-M\frac{N^{\varphi}Y^{\sigma}}{A}\left(\varphi\hat{n}_{t+k}+\sigma\hat{y}_{t+k}-\hat{a}_{t+k}+\log\Pi_{t-1,t+k}\right)\right]
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

Note that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left(\frac{N_{t+k}^{\varphi}Y_{t+k}^{\sigma}}{A_{t+k}}\right)
\]

\end_inset

is the 
\emph on
real
\emph default
 marginal cost in period 
\begin_inset Formula $t+k$
\end_inset

 for a firm whose price was last set in period 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 Since
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
P_{t}^{*} & = & M\cdot(\mbox{nominal marginal cost)}\\
\frac{P_{t}^{*}}{P_{t}} & = & M\cdot\left(\frac{N_{t}^{\varphi}Y_{t}^{\sigma}}{A_{t}}\right)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

then, in the steady state with zero inflation (i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{t}^{*}=P_{t}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

), we have 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left[1-M\left(\frac{N^{\varphi}Y^{\sigma}}{A}\right)\right]=0\iff M\left(\frac{N^{\varphi}Y^{\sigma}}{A}\right)=1\iff\left(\frac{N^{\varphi}Y^{\sigma}}{A}\right)=\frac{1}{M}
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

As a result, we can rewrite equation (25) as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \sum_{k=0}^{\infty}}\theta^{k}E_{t}\left\{ \beta^{k}Y\left[p_{t}^{*}-p_{t-1}-\left(\hat{mc}_{t+k}+p_{t+k}-p_{t-1}\right)\right]\right\} =0
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{mc}_{t+k}\equiv\varphi\hat{n}_{t+k}+\sigma\hat{y}_{t+k}-\hat{a}_{t+k}=mc_{t+k}-mc$
\end_inset

 denotes the log-deviation of the marginal cost from its steady state value
 
\begin_inset Formula $mc=-\mu$
\end_inset

, and where 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu\equiv\log M$
\end_inset

 is the log of the desired gross markup (which, for 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

 close to one, is approximately equal to the net markup 
\begin_inset Formula $M-1$
\end_inset

).
 After rearranging terms, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \sum_{k=0}^{\infty}}\left(\beta\theta\right)^{k}(p_{t}^{*}-p_{t-1})={\displaystyle \sum_{k=0}^{\infty}}\left(\beta\theta\right)^{k}E_{t}\left\{ \hat{mc}_{t+k}+\left(p_{t+k}-p_{t-1}\right)\right\} 
\]

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $0<\beta\theta<1$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\left(\beta\theta\right)^{k}=\frac{1}{(1-\beta\theta)}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
p_{t}^{*}-p_{t-1}=(1-\beta\theta){\displaystyle \sum_{k=0}^{\infty}}(\beta\theta)^{k}E_{t}\left\{ \hat{mc}_{t+k}+\left(p_{t+k}-p_{t-1}\right)\right\} 
\end{equation}

\end_inset

If marginal cost is high, then a firm will set its price high and above
 marginal cost.
 Also, if the general price level, 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{t+k}-p_{t-1}$
\end_inset

, is high, then the firm will also set a higher price.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In order to gain some additional intuition about the factors determining
 a firm's price-setting decision, it is useful to rewrite (34) as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p_{t}^{*}=\mu+(1-\beta\theta){\displaystyle \sum_{k=0}^{\infty}}(\beta\theta)^{k}E_{t}\{mc_{t+k}+p_{t+k}\}
\]

\end_inset

Hence, firms resetting their prices will choose a price that corresponds
 to the desired markup over a weighted average of their currenct and expected
 (
\emph on
nominal
\emph default
) marginal costs, with the weights being proportional to the probability
 of the price remaining effective at each horizon 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta^{k}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For a moment, we will turn our attention towards the remaining equations
 in the system.
 First, we focus on equation (26), which illustrates the aggregate price
 dyanmics.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\Pi_{t}^{1-\epsilon} & = & \theta+(1-\theta)\left(\frac{P_{t}^{*}}{P_{t-1}}\right)^{1-\epsilon}\nonumber \\
(1-\epsilon)\pi_{t} & = & (1-\theta)(1-\epsilon)\left(p_{t}^{*}-p_{t-1}\right)\nonumber \\
\pi_{t} & = & (1-\theta)(p_{t}^{*}-p_{t-1})
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

Equation (35) makes clear that, in the present setup, inflation results
 from the fact that firms reoptimizing in any given period choose a price
 that differs from the economy's average price in the previous period.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Moving on, we consider equation (27).
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
N_{t} & = & {\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}N_{t}(i)\ di\\
 & = & {\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}\frac{Y_{t}(i)}{A_{t}}\ d_{i}\\
 & = & {\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}\frac{1}{A_{t}}\left(\frac{P_{t}(i)}{P_{t}}\right)^{-\epsilon}Y_{t}\ di\\
 & = & \frac{Y_{t}}{A_{t}}{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}\left(\frac{P_{t}(i)}{P_{t}}\right)^{-\epsilon}di
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

where the third equality follows from equations (9) and (10) and the goods
 market clearing condition.
 Taking 
\begin_inset Formula $\log$
\end_inset

 on both sides,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
n_{t}=y_{t}-a_{t}+d_{t}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $d_{t}\equiv\log\left[{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}\left(\frac{P_{t}(i)}{P_{t}}\right)^{-\epsilon}di\right]$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $di$
\end_inset

 is a measure of price (and, hence, output) dispersion across firms.
 In a neighborhood of the zero inflation steady state, 
\begin_inset Formula $d_{t}$
\end_inset

 is equal to zero up to a first-order approximation.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}\left(\frac{P_{t}(i)}{P_{t}}\right)^{-\epsilon}di & = & {\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}\exp\left[-\epsilon\left(p_{t}(i)-p_{t}\right)\right]di\\
 & \approx & {\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}\left[1-\epsilon(p_{t}(i)-p_{t})\right]di\\
 & = & 1-\left[\epsilon{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}\left(p_{t}(i)-p_{t}\right)d_{i}\right]
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{t}=\left[{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}P_{t}(i)^{1-\epsilon}di\right]^{\frac{1}{1-\epsilon}}$
\end_inset

, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
1 & = & \int_{0}^{1}\left(\frac{P_{t}(i)}{P_{t}}\right)^{1-\epsilon}d_{i}\\
 & = & \int_{0}^{1}\exp\left[(1-\epsilon)(p_{t}(i)-p_{t})\right]d_{i}\\
1 & \approx & {\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}\left[1+(1-\epsilon)(p_{t}(i)-p_{t})\right]d_{i}\\
0 & \approx & (1-\epsilon){\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}(p_{t}(i)-p_{t})di
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon>1$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $(1-\epsilon)\neq0$
\end_inset

, so we must have 
\begin_inset Formula ${\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}(p_{t}(i)-p_{t})di=0$
\end_inset

.
 Hence,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
dt\equiv\log\left[{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}\left(\frac{P_{t}(i)}{P_{t}}\right)^{-\epsilon}di\right]\approx\log(1)=0
\]

\end_inset

Thus, one can write the following approximate relation between aggregate
 output, employment, and technology
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{t}=a_{t}+n_{t}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To summarize, we have derived the following three equations
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
p_{t}^{*} & = & (1-\beta\theta){\displaystyle \sum_{k=0}^{\infty}}\left(\beta\theta\right)^{k}E_{t}\left\{ \hat{mc}_{t+k}+p_{t+k}\right\} \\
\pi_{t} & = & (1-\theta)(p_{t}^{*}-p_{t-1})\\
y_{t} & = & a_{t}+n_{t}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

i.e., equations (34), (35), and (36), which we use to rewrite the optimal
 price setting equation.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
p_{t}^{*} & = & (1-\beta\theta){\displaystyle \sum_{k=0}^{\infty}}\left(\beta\theta\right)^{k}E_{t}\left\{ \hat{mc}_{t+k}+p_{t+k}\right\} \\
 & = & (1-\beta\theta)\left(\hat{mc}_{t}+p_{t}\right)+(1-\beta\theta){\displaystyle \sum_{k=1}^{\infty}}(\beta\theta)^{k}E_{t}\left\{ \hat{mc}_{t+k}+p_{t+k}\right\} \\
 & = & (1-\beta\theta)\left(\hat{mc}_{t}+p_{t}\right)+(\beta\theta)E_{t}\left\{ (1-\beta\theta){\displaystyle \sum_{k=0}^{\infty}}(\beta\theta)^{k}E_{t+1}\left[\hat{mc}_{t+1+k}+p_{t+1+k}\right]\right\} 
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Important to note, that we use the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

power rule
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 for expectations, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{t}\left\{ E_{t+1}\left[x_{t+k}\right]\right\} =E_{t}\left\{ x_{t+k}\right\} $
\end_inset

.
 Hence, the optimal price setting equation can be rewritten as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p_{t}^{*}=(1-\beta\theta)(\hat{mc}_{t}+p_{t})+\beta\theta E_{t}\left\{ p_{t+1}^{*}\right\} 
\]

\end_inset

is a weighted average.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

A firm reoptimizing in period 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 will set a price higher if marginal cost and/or the relative price is high.
 Furthermore, since the firm may not be able to reset its price again in
 period 
\begin_inset Formula $t+1$
\end_inset

, then it must also take into account its expected optimal price for next
 period.
 So, if a firm expects the general price level to be higher in period 
\begin_inset Formula $t+1$
\end_inset

, then the firm will set a higher optimal price in period 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 as insurance since it may not be able to change its price in the next period.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Subtracting 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{t-1}$
\end_inset

 from both sides yields
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
p_{t}^{*}-p_{t-1}=(1-\beta\theta)\hat{mc}_{t}+\pi_{t}+\beta\theta E_{t}\left\{ p_{t+1}^{*}-p_{t}\right\} 
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Equation (35) implies
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\frac{\pi_{t}}{(1-\theta)} & = & (1-\beta\theta)\hat{mc}_{t}+\pi_{t}+\beta\theta E_{t}\left\{ \frac{\pi_{t+1}}{(1-\theta)}\right\} \nonumber \\
\pi_{t}-(1-\theta)\pi_{t} & = & (1-\theta)(1-\beta\theta)\hat{mc}_{t}+\beta\theta E_{t}\left\{ \pi_{t+1}\right\} \nonumber \\
\pi_{t} & = & \frac{(1-\theta)(1-\beta\theta)}{\theta}\hat{mc}_{t}+\beta E_{t}\left\{ \pi_{t+1}\right\} \nonumber \\
\pi_{t} & = & \lambda\hat{mc}_{t}+\beta E_{t}\left\{ \pi_{t+1}\right\} 
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda\equiv\frac{(1-\theta)(1-\beta\theta)}{\theta}$
\end_inset

.
 This equation determines inflation dynamics.
 Current inflation rate depends on the current marginal cost and the discounted
 expected inflation rate in the next period.
 So, if a higher inflation rate is expected in 
\begin_inset Formula $t+1$
\end_inset

, then current inflation will increase as a result
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

When 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 is high, the probability of firms being able to change their prices is
 low.
 Consequently, firms change their prices less often signifying that prices
 are 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

stickier.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 Important to note, 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

 is strictly decreasing in the index of price stickiness 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, a large rise in marginal cost does not have a substantial impact
 on the inflation rate.
 So, in the case of 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 being large, when a firm does have the opportunity to change its price,
 the firm will choose not to deviate greatly from the prices of its competitors
 because it is trying to keep its relative price ratio in line with the
 other firms.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Solving (38) forward, inflation is expressed as the discounted sum of current
 and expected future deviations of 
\emph on
real
\emph default
 marginal costs from the steady state
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\pi_{t}=\lambda{\displaystyle \sum_{k=0}^{\infty}}\beta^{k}E_{t}\left\{ \hat{mc}_{t+k}\right\} 
\]

\end_inset

Equivalently, and defining the average markup in the economy as 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{t}=-mc_{t}$
\end_inset

, it is seen that inflation will be high when firms expect average markups
 to be below their steady state (i.e., desired) level 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset

, for in that case firms that have the opportunity to reset prices will
 choose a price above the economy's average price level in order to realign
 their markup closer to its desired level.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

It is worth emphasizing here that the mechanism underlying fluctuations
 in the aggregate price level and inflation as laid out above have little
 in common with the mechanism at work in the classical monetary economy.
 Thus, in the present model, inflation results from the aggregate consequences
 of purposeful price-setting decisions by firms, which adjust their prices
 in light of current and anticipated cost conditions.
 By contrast, in the classical monetary economy analyzed in Set 3, inflation
 is a consequence of the changes in the aggregate price level that, given
 the monetary policy rule in place, are required in order to support an
 equilibrium allocation that is independent of the evolution of nominal
 variables, with no account given of the mechanism (other than an invisible
 hand) that will bring about those price level changes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Next, we hypothesize that the output gap is related to marginal cost.
 If the output is overheated above the reference level, i.e., the natural
 output level, then marginal cost will be higher than its reference point.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\hat{mc}_{t} & = & \varphi\hat{n}_{t}+\sigma\hat{y}_{t}-\hat{a}_{t}\\
 & = & \varphi n_{t}+\sigma y_{t}-a_{t}-(\varphi n+\sigma y-a)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset

 are the log steady state values, so 
\begin_inset Formula $(\varphi n+\sigma y-a)$
\end_inset

 is the log steady state marginal cost.
 Since the steady state value of marginal cost is 
\begin_inset Formula $mc=-\mu$
\end_inset

, then the above equation becomes
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{mc}_{t}=\varphi n_{t}+\sigma y_{t}-a_{t}+\mu
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu\equiv\log M$
\end_inset

.
 Recall that in a flexible price equilibrium, 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{P_{t}(i)}{P_{t}}=1$
\end_inset

, and thus,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
1 & = & M\frac{\left(N_{t}^{n}\right)^{\varphi}\left(Y_{t}^{n}\right)^{\sigma}}{A_{t}}\\
0 & = & \varphi n_{t}^{n}+\sigma y_{t}^{n}-a_{t}+\mu\\
\mu & = & -\varphi n_{t}^{n}-\sigma y_{t}^{n}+a_{t}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

We plug this expression for 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset

 into the equation for marginal cost.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\hat{mc}_{t} & = & \varphi n_{t}+\sigma y_{t}-a_{t}-\left(\varphi n_{t}^{n}+\sigma y_{t}^{n}-a_{t}\right)\\
 & = & \varphi(n_{t}-n_{t}^{n})+\sigma(y_{t}-y_{t}^{n})
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}=a_{t}+n_{t}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}^{n}=a_{t}+n_{t}^{n}$
\end_inset

, then we obtain
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\hat{mc}_{t} & = & \varphi(y_{t}-a_{t}-y_{t}^{n}+a_{t})+\sigma\tilde{y}_{t}\nonumber \\
 & = & \varphi\tilde{y}_{t}+\sigma\tilde{y}_{t}\nonumber \\
\hat{mc} & = & (\varphi+\sigma)\tilde{y}_{t}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

i.e., the log deviation of real marginal cost from its steady state is proportiona
l to the output gap.
 When the economy is 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

overheated
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 relative to the adjusted benchmark (i.e., the natural rate of output, which
 accounts for the technology level), then the marginal cost will be high,
 relative to some benchmark.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

By combining equation (39) with equation (38), one can obtain an equation
 relating inflation to its one period ahead forecast and the output gap
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\pi_{t}=\beta E_{t}\left\{ \pi_{t+1}\right\} +\kappa\tilde{y}_{t}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\kappa\equiv\lambda(\sigma+\varphi)$
\end_inset

.
 Equation (40) is ofter referred to as the 
\series bold
New Keynesian Phillips curve
\series default
 (or NKPC, for short), and constitutes one of the key building blocks of
 the basic New Keynesian model.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Summary of Results
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The Dynamic IS Equation and the New Keynesian Phillips curve together with
 an equilibrium process for the natural rate 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{t}^{n}$
\end_inset

 (which in general will depend on all the real exogenous forces in the model,
 i.e., the technology shocks) constitute the non-policy block of the basic
 New Keynesian model.
 That block has a simple recursive structure: The NKPC determines inflation
 given a path for the output gap, whereas the DIS equation determines the
 output gap given a path for the (exogenous) natural rate and the actual
 real rate.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In order to close the model, supplement those two equations with one or
 more equations determining how the nominal interest rate 
\begin_inset Formula $i_{t}$
\end_inset

 evolves over time, i.e., a description of how monetary policy is conducted.
 One such equation is known as the 
\series bold
Taylor Rule
\series default
, which is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
i_{t}=\rho+\phi_{\pi}\pi_{t}+\phi_{y}\tilde{y}_{t}+\nu_{t}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\nu_{t}$
\end_inset

 has been included to account for monetary policy shocks.
 It is one interest rate rule that the central bank can follow to determine
 the nominal interest rate.
 Assume 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{\pi}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{y}$
\end_inset

 are non-negative coefficients, chosen by the monetary authority.
 Note that the choice of the intercept 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho$
\end_inset

 makes the rule consistent with a zero inflation steady state.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Now, we have a system of 3 equations, 3 endogenous variables 
\begin_inset Formula $(\tilde{y}_{t},\pi_{t},i_{t})$
\end_inset

, and 2 exogenous shocks 
\begin_inset Formula $(r_{t}^{n},\nu_{t})$
\end_inset

.
 All of the remaining endogenous variables in the model can be explained
 by relating them to one or more of the three equations and endogenous variables
 above.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Thus, and in contrast with the classical model analyzed in Set 3, when prices
 are sticky the equilibrium path of real variables cannot be determined
 independently of monetary policy.
 In other words, monetary policy is non-neutral.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Economic Intuition
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Dynamic IS curve
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\tilde{y}_{t}=E_{t}\left\{ \tilde{y}_{t+1}\right\} -\frac{1}{\sigma}\left(i_{t}-E_{t}\left\{ \pi_{t+1}\right\} -r_{t}^{n}\right)
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If real interest is higher than some benchmark, then the households will
 decrease consumption in period 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, and instead reallocate the resources to purchase more bonds (i.e., households
 will 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

save
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

).
 This is essentially another version of the Euler equation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*

\series bold
New Keynesian Phillips Curve
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\pi_{t}=\kappa\tilde{y}_{t}+\beta E_{t}\left\{ \pi_{t+1}\right\} 
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

When a firm sets its price, it considers the relative price (the price it
 sets compared to its peers, i.e., the price index) and marginal cost.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\kappa$
\end_inset

 captures how long the firm must keep the price it sets before it can change
 it again.
 If production is higher than the benchmark (higher than the current technology
 dictates), then households must supply more labor and suffer more disutility.
 To compensate the households, the firms must increase wages, and as a result,
 marginal cost will be higher, which will lead to higher prices and inflation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*

\series bold
Taylor Rule
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
i_{t}=\rho+\phi_{\pi}\pi_{t}+\phi_{y}\tilde{y}_{t}+\nu_{t}
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Under the assumption of non-negative coefficients 
\begin_inset Formula $(\phi_{\pi},\phi_{y})$
\end_inset

, it can be shown that a necessary and sufficient condition for uniqueness
 is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\kappa(\phi_{\pi}-1)+(1-\beta)\phi_{y}>0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

[Bullard and Mitra (2002)], which is assumed to hold, unless stated otherwise.
 Hence, the economy is stable around the steady state (i.e., the natural level
 of the endogenous variables), if and only if condition (42) is satisfied.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To interpret this condition, let us ignore 
\begin_inset Formula $(1-\beta)\phi_{y}$
\end_inset

 because 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta\approx1$
\end_inset

.
 Then, we get
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\phi_{\pi}-1>0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which is known as the 
\series bold
Taylor Principle
\series default
.
 Thus, if the central bank follows this recommendation, then a 1% increase
 in inflation must lead to a more than 1% increase in nominal interest rate.
 Consequently, real interest rate will rise, which, in turn, prevents the
 emergence of multiple equilibria.
 Under the Taylor Rule, the model dictates that the central bank must fight
 inflation so as to keep the economy stable and prevent multiple equilibria.
\end_layout

